<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Courland Tobago</h1><p class="st-meta">1654 CE – 1689 CE · capital Jacobusstadt</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Caribbean colony of the Duchy of Courland on Tobago, settled in 1654 and fought over with the Dutch until abandoned near the end of the century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler">Duke Jacob Kettler</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a> sent settlers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> in 1654 aboard the ship Das Wappen der Herzogin von Kurland. They landed at what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-courland-bay">Great Courland Bay</a>, built Fort Jacobus and the settlement of Jacobusstadt, and named the island New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colonists, including Latvian peasants and German officers, tried to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, tobacco and other tropical crops with enslaved African labour. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlement on the other side of the island, backed by the Lampsins brothers, competed with the Courlanders, and both struggled with disease and shortage of settlers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took over in 1659 while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler">Duke Jacob</a> was a Swedish prisoner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> regained the claim and resettled the island in the 1680s, but the colony was abandoned by about 1690. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> later became British; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-courland-bay">Great Courland Bay</a> keeps the name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1654 CE – 1689 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.62%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1654 CE – 1689 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jacobusstadt</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jacob Kettler</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latvian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Courland, Neu-Kurland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1672&amp;focus=courland-tobago" aria-label="Courland Tobago on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2999 1771 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Courland Tobago lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5513 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3319 2027h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3319 2027h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Trinidad and Tobago. <a href="/atlas?year=1672&amp;focus=courland-tobago">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

